module tecs.input

--[[
Keyboard, pointer, touch and gamepad state folded from typed platform events.

Live state survives frame boundaries. Frame edges clear before each sealed host
batch, while latched edges accumulate until the first fixed step consumes
them. Blocking layers move capture and clear pending edges without inventing
releases for controls that remain physically held.

Gamepads reach the same fold through a different door. The keyboard, the
pointer and touch arrive in the host's sealed batch, because the window system
owns them; a gamepad belongs to no window, so `tecs.platform.gamepadbackend`
is polled once a frame by the `tecs.PollGamepads` system `install` adds, and
what it saw becomes the same typed platform events the host would have sent.
That is why `handleEvent` is the only fold in this module: a synthetic gamepad
event from the debug server and a real one from a controller take one path.

Buttons use positional names. `"south"` is the button nearest the player on
every pad, where `"a"` is the one nearest the player on some pads and the one
to its right on others.

```nupp
local pad <const> = state:gamepad(1)
if pad ~= nil and pad.connected then
    local moveX <const> = pad:axis("leftX")
    if pad:buttonPressed("south") then
        print(moveX)
    end
end
```

## Device features and where they went

The SDL implementation exposed more of a controller than any portable Rust
source does, so each of those is decided here rather than left to be
rediscovered.

Retained: positional buttons, sticks, analog triggers, hot plug, the power
state, and rumble. Rumble is retained conditionally, which the contract already
allowed for: `gilrs` implements force feedback on `evdev` and XInput and not on
`IOKit`, so `Gamepad.rumble` answers false on macOS the way it always answered
false for a device without motors.

Retained on the platforms that have it: touch, which `winit` reports on
Windows, Linux, Android and the web and documents as unsupported on macOS,
where there is no touch screen to report. Touch pressure comes through where the
surface measures it and reads zero where it does not.

Deferred: the pen. `winit` 0.30 translates no pen event at all, and its 0.31
line folds pens into a unified pointer with a `Pen` kind. Waiting for that is
cheaper and more correct than writing `NSEvent`, `WM_POINTER` and XInput2 tablet
handling in three places for a surface with no consumer in this tree. The
contract to restore is the eight `pen` event kinds, the `penState` argument on
the debug server's `send_event`, and pressure, tilt and eraser state on this
record.

Removed: gamepad motion sensors, gamepad touchpads, trigger rumble, the light
bar, the player-index slot, the hardware button label, the device family and
device path, the remap event, and standalone motion sensors. Every one of them
is vendor HID parsing that no cross-platform Rust crate does, and the
positional vocabulary above is what a game actually binds against. A game that
wants a `"cross"` glyph rather than a `"south"` name reads the pad's `name` and
chooses its own art.
]]

local data = require("tecs.data")
local ecs = require("tecs.ecs")
local gamepadbackend = require("tecs.platform.gamepadbackend")
local log = require("tecs.log")
local platformevents = require("tecs.platform.events")

--- Compatibility surface: a developer types this logger name into a filter, so
--- it is configuration rather than an identifier and does not move.
local LOGGER = log.get(log.INPUT)

--- A position in the input capture stack.
export record Layer
    name: string
    blocking: boolean
    index: integer
end

--- A conventional pointer button name.
export type MouseButton = "left" | "middle" | "right" | "back" | "forward"

--- A positional gamepad button name.
---
--- Compatibility surface: these are the names the Teal implementation
--- published, so a saved binding and a game keep working across the move. They
--- are positional rather than Xbox-only on purpose: the hardware label is a
--- separate question, and one this engine no longer answers.
export type GamepadButton = "south"
    | "east"
    | "west"
    | "north"
    | "back"
    | "guide"
    | "start"
    | "leftStick"
    | "rightStick"
    | "leftShoulder"
    | "rightShoulder"
    | "dpadUp"
    | "dpadDown"
    | "dpadLeft"
    | "dpadRight"

--- A gamepad axis name.
---
--- Compatibility surface: these are the names the Teal implementation
--- published. A stick runs -1 to 1 with `leftY` and `rightY` growing downward,
--- and a trigger runs zero to one.
export type GamepadAxis = "leftX" | "leftY" | "rightX" | "rightY" | "leftTrigger" | "rightTrigger"

--- One finger on a touch surface, from the most recent touch event.
---
--- Input owns and reuses every field. Callers treat the record as read-only and
--- copy values they need to retain.
export record Touch
    --- Read-only. Names the touch surface's opaque identity, set when the
    --- finger first appears.
    device: string

    --- Read-only. Names the finger's opaque identity, set when it first
    --- appears.
    finger: string

    --- Read-only. Reports the position in logical window coordinates.
    x: number

    --- Read-only. Reports the position in logical window coordinates.
    y: number

    --- Read-only. Reports the position across the surface, from zero to one.
    normalX: number

    --- Read-only. Reports the position down the surface, from zero to one.
    normalY: number

    --- Read-only. Reports the pressure from zero to one, and zero where the
    --- surface does not measure it.
    pressure: number
end

--- One attached gamepad's identity, event state and outputs.
---
--- Input owns the object. A retained reference stays safe after the device goes
--- away: queries answer with neutral values, `rumble` returns false, and
--- `connected` stays false. A reconnect creates a new object rather than
--- reviving the old one.
export record Gamepad
    --- Read-only. Names the device, and matches the `which` field of every
    --- gamepad platform event about it.
    id: integer

    --- Read-only. Reports true from the moment Input opens the device until it
    --- goes away, and false permanently afterwards.
    connected: boolean

    --- Read-only. Reports the platform's display name for the device, and an
    --- empty string when it reports none. Saved bindings use `guid`.
    name: string

    --- Read-only. Reports the stable identity a saved binding matches on, and
    --- an empty string when the platform reports none.
    guid: string

    private backend: gamepadbackend.Backend

    --- Reads the shared layer stack. A layer blocks every device or none, so
    --- this answers from the owning input state rather than from the pad.
    private gateRead: function(layer: Layer?): boolean

    --- Reports whether queries should answer with fixed-step latched edges.
    private gateFixed: function(): boolean

    private down: {[integer]: boolean}
    private pressed: {[integer]: boolean}
    private released: {[integer]: boolean}
    private latchedPressed: {[integer]: boolean}
    private latchedReleased: {[integer]: boolean}

    --- Latest value per axis. An axis no event has mentioned reads as zero.
    private axisValues: {[integer]: number}

    buttonDown: function(borrows self: Gamepad, button: GamepadButton | integer, layer: Layer?): boolean
    buttonPressed: function(borrows self: Gamepad, button: GamepadButton | integer, layer: Layer?): boolean
    buttonReleased: function(borrows self: Gamepad, button: GamepadButton | integer, layer: Layer?): boolean
    axis: function(borrows self: Gamepad, axis: GamepadAxis | integer, deadzone: number?, layer: Layer?): number
    hasButton: function(borrows self: Gamepad, button: GamepadButton | integer): boolean
    hasAxis: function(borrows self: Gamepad, axis: GamepadAxis | integer): boolean
    power: function(borrows self: Gamepad): (gamepadbackend.PowerState, integer)
    rumble: function(borrows self: Gamepad, low: number, high: number, seconds: number): boolean
end

--- Live, frame-edge, and fixed-latched keyboard and pointer input.
export record Input
    mouseX: number
    mouseY: number
    mouseDeltaX: number
    mouseDeltaY: number
    wheelX: number
    wheelY: number
    wheelTicksX: integer
    wheelTicksY: integer
    text: string

    private keysDown: {[string]: boolean}
    private keysPressed: {[string]: boolean}
    private keysReleased: {[string]: boolean}
    private keysLatchedPressed: {[string]: boolean}
    private keysLatchedReleased: {[string]: boolean}
    private mouseButtonsDown: {[integer]: boolean}
    private mouseButtonsPressed: {[integer]: boolean}
    private mouseButtonsReleased: {[integer]: boolean}
    private mouseButtonsLatchedPressed: {[integer]: boolean}
    private mouseButtonsLatchedReleased: {[integer]: boolean}
    private modifierMask: integer
    private fixed: boolean
    private layers: {Layer}
    private enterFixedSystem: ecs.System?
    private exitFixedSystem: ecs.System?
    private pollSystem: ecs.System?
    private backend: gamepadbackend.Backend
    private pads: {[integer]: Gamepad}
    private padList: {Gamepad}

    --- The drain buffer, allocated once because the seam is crossed per frame.
    private padEvents: {gamepadbackend.Event}

    private fingers: {[string]: Touch}

    beginFrame: function(exclusive self: Input): nil
    enterFixedPhase: function(exclusive self: Input): nil
    exitFixedPhase: function(exclusive self: Input): nil
    handleEvent: function(exclusive self: Input, event: platformevents.Event): nil
    keyDown: function(borrows self: Input, key: string, layer: Layer?): boolean
    keyPressed: function(borrows self: Input, key: string, layer: Layer?): boolean
    keyReleased: function(borrows self: Input, key: string, layer: Layer?): boolean
    modifiers: function(borrows self: Input): integer
    mouseDown: function(borrows self: Input, button: MouseButton | integer, layer: Layer?): boolean
    mousePressed: function(borrows self: Input, button: MouseButton | integer, layer: Layer?): boolean
    mouseReleased: function(borrows self: Input, button: MouseButton | integer, layer: Layer?): boolean
    pushLayer: function(exclusive self: Input, name: string, blocking: boolean?): Layer
    popLayer: function(exclusive self: Input): Layer?
    topLayer: function(borrows self: Input): Layer
    canRead: function(borrows self: Input, layer: Layer?): boolean
    touches: function(borrows self: Input, layer: Layer?): {Touch}
    gamepads: function(borrows self: Input): {Gamepad}
    gamepad: function(borrows self: Input, index: integer?): Gamepad?
    gamepadById: function(borrows self: Input, id: integer): Gamepad?
    refreshDevices: function(exclusive self: Input): nil
    pollDevices: function(exclusive self: Input): {platformevents.Event}
    destroy: function(exclusive self: Input): nil
end

--- The code each positional button name resolves to.
---
--- The numbers are the seam's own and match `native/rust/tecs-gamepad`. The
--- names are the compatibility surface; the numbers never left the process.
local BUTTON_CODES: {
    [string]: integer
} = {
    south = 1,
    east = 2,
    west = 3,
    north = 4,
    back = 5,
    guide = 6,
    start = 7,
    leftStick = 8,
    rightStick = 9,
    leftShoulder = 10,
    rightShoulder = 11,
    dpadUp = 12,
    dpadDown = 13,
    dpadLeft = 14,
    dpadRight = 15,
}

--- The code each axis name resolves to.
local AXIS_CODES: {
    [string]: integer
} = {leftX = 1, leftY = 2, rightX = 3, rightY = 4, leftTrigger = 5, rightTrigger = 6,}

--- The magnitude below which a stick reads as centered.
---
--- Hardware rests a little off center, so a game that did not subtract
--- something here would drift. The value is the one the Teal implementation
--- used, and `Gamepad.axis` takes an override for a game that wants its own.
local DEFAULT_DEADZONE: number = 0.15

--- The greatest number of observations one drain crossing carries.
local DRAIN_CAPACITY: integer = 64

--- The gamepad source this process reads, resolved once.
---
--- One per process rather than one per world, because the platform's device
--- source is one thing and opening it twice would mean two hot-plug streams
--- reporting the same controller under two identities.
local sharedBackend: gamepadbackend.Backend? = nil

--- Returns the process-wide gamepad source, opening it on first use.
---
--- A build with no `libtecsgamepad` answers with the device-free backend, in
--- the same shape and with the same silence a machine with no controller
--- support produces.
--- @return the shared backend
local function devices(): gamepadbackend.Backend
    local existing = sharedBackend
    if existing ~= nil then
        return existing
    end
    local opened: gamepadbackend.Backend? = nil
    local loaded, native = pcall(require, "tecs.platform.gamepadnative")
    if loaded then
        local backend, failure = (native as {[string]: any}).open()
        opened = backend as gamepadbackend.Backend?
        if opened == nil then
            LOGGER:info("no gamepad source: %s", tostring(failure))
        end
    else
        LOGGER:info("no gamepad backend: %s", tostring(native))
    end
    if opened == nil then
        opened = gamepadbackend.none()
    end
    sharedBackend = opened

    return opened
end

local worldInputKey: data.Key<Input> = data.newKey("tecs.input", nil)

--- Clears one pad's frame-local edges.
local function padBeginFrame(exclusive pad: Gamepad): nil
    pad.pressed = {}
    pad.released = {}
end

--- Consumes one pad's latched edges at the end of a fixed step.
local function padExitFixed(exclusive pad: Gamepad): nil
    pad.latchedPressed = {}
    pad.latchedReleased = {}
end

--- Clears every edge a pad holds, in both tiers.
local function padClearEdges(exclusive pad: Gamepad): nil
    pad.pressed = {}
    pad.released = {}
    pad.latchedPressed = {}
    pad.latchedReleased = {}
end

--- Releases everything a pad holds without inventing a physical release.
---
--- A control held as the window loses focus never sends its own release, so a
--- game would read it as held until the player pressed it again somewhere the
--- application could see.
local function padReleaseHeld(exclusive pad: Gamepad): nil
    for code in pairs(pad.down) do
        pad.released[code] = true
        pad.latchedReleased[code] = true
    end
    pad.down = {}
    pad.axisValues = {}
end

--- Records that a device is gone, leaving retained references harmless.
local function padDisconnect(exclusive pad: Gamepad): nil
    pad.connected = false
    pad.down = {}
    pad.axisValues = {}
    padClearEdges(pad)
end

local function clearEdges(exclusive input: Input): nil
    input.keysPressed = {}
    input.keysReleased = {}
    input.keysLatchedPressed = {}
    input.keysLatchedReleased = {}
    input.mouseButtonsPressed = {}
    input.mouseButtonsReleased = {}
    input.mouseButtonsLatchedPressed = {}
    input.mouseButtonsLatchedReleased = {}
    input.mouseDeltaX = 0
    input.mouseDeltaY = 0
    input.wheelX = 0
    input.wheelY = 0
    input.wheelTicksX = 0
    input.wheelTicksY = 0
    input.text = ""
    for index = 1, #input.padList do
        padClearEdges(input.padList[index])
    end
end

local function readableFrom(borrows input: Input): integer
    for index = #input.layers, 1, -1 do
        if input.layers[index].blocking then
            return index
        end
    end

    return 1
end

local function releaseHeld(exclusive input: Input): nil
    for key in pairs(input.keysDown) do
        input.keysReleased[key] = true
        input.keysLatchedReleased[key] = true
    end
    for button in pairs(input.mouseButtonsDown) do
        input.mouseButtonsReleased[button] = true
        input.mouseButtonsLatchedReleased[button] = true
    end
    input.keysDown = {}
    input.mouseButtonsDown = {}
    input.modifierMask = 0
    for index = 1, #input.padList do
        padReleaseHeld(input.padList[index])
    end
    input.fingers = {}
end

local function mouseCode(button: MouseButton | integer): integer
    if button is integer then
        if button < 1 then
            error("input: mouse button must be positive", 3)
        end
        return button
    elseif button == "left" then
        return 1
    elseif button == "middle" then
        return 2
    elseif button == "right" then
        return 3
    elseif button == "back" then
        return 4
    elseif button == "forward" then
        return 5
    end

    error("input: unknown mouse button", 3)
end

local function buttonCode(button: GamepadButton | integer): integer
    if button is integer then
        if button < 1 then
            error("input: gamepad button must be positive", 3)
        end

        return button
    end
    local code = BUTTON_CODES[button]
    if code == nil then
        error("input: unknown gamepad button", 3)
    end

    return code
end

local function axisCode(axis: GamepadAxis | integer): integer
    if axis is integer then
        if axis < 1 then
            error("input: gamepad axis must be positive", 3)
        end

        return axis
    end
    local code = AXIS_CODES[axis]
    if code == nil then
        error("input: unknown gamepad axis", 3)
    end

    return code
end

--- Creates empty input state with the readable base layer.
---
--- The state reads no device unless it is given a source. Constructing input
--- state by hand is what a test and a headless tool do, and neither should
--- start the platform's device enumeration as a side effect: on macOS that is a
--- run loop on a thread of its own, and a suite whose result changes when
--- somebody plugs a controller in is worse than one that reads no controller at
--- all. `install` is the call that reaches real hardware.
--- @param backend the gamepad source, defaulting to no devices at all
--- @return a standalone input state
export function newInput(backend: gamepadbackend.Backend?): Input
    local base = new Layer(name = "base", blocking = true, index = 1)
    local buffer: {gamepadbackend.Event} = {}
    for index = 1, DRAIN_CAPACITY do
        buffer[index] = gamepadbackend.newEvent()
    end
    local result = new Input(
        mouseX = 0,
        mouseY = 0,
        mouseDeltaX = 0,
        mouseDeltaY = 0,
        wheelX = 0,
        wheelY = 0,
        wheelTicksX = 0,
        wheelTicksY = 0,
        text = "",
        keysDown = {},
        keysPressed = {},
        keysReleased = {},
        keysLatchedPressed = {},
        keysLatchedReleased = {},
        mouseButtonsDown = {},
        mouseButtonsPressed = {},
        mouseButtonsReleased = {},
        mouseButtonsLatchedPressed = {},
        mouseButtonsLatchedReleased = {},
        modifierMask = 0,
        fixed = false,
        layers = {base},
        enterFixedSystem = nil,
        exitFixedSystem = nil,
        pollSystem = nil,
        backend = backend ?? gamepadbackend.none(),
        pads = {},
        padList = {},
        padEvents = buffer,
        fingers = {}
    )
    result.enterFixedSystem = function(_dt: number, exclusive _world: ecs.World): nil
        result:enterFixedPhase()
    end
    result.exitFixedSystem = function(_dt: number, exclusive _world: ecs.World): nil
        result:exitFixedPhase()
    end
    result.pollSystem = function(_dt: number, exclusive world: ecs.World): nil
        local produced = result:pollDevices()
        for index = 1, #produced do
            platformevents.dispatch(world, produced[index])
        end
    end

    return result
end

--- Starts a frame by clearing frame-local edges and accumulated deltas.
--- @param self the input state receiving the next event batch
function Input.beginFrame(exclusive self: Input): nil
    self.keysPressed = {}
    self.keysReleased = {}
    self.mouseButtonsPressed = {}
    self.mouseButtonsReleased = {}
    self.mouseDeltaX = 0
    self.mouseDeltaY = 0
    self.wheelX = 0
    self.wheelY = 0
    self.wheelTicksX = 0
    self.wheelTicksY = 0
    self.text = ""
    for index = 1, #self.padList do
        padBeginFrame(self.padList[index])
    end
end

--- Selects latched edges for queries inside one fixed step.
--- @param self the installed input state
function Input.enterFixedPhase(exclusive self: Input): nil
    self.fixed = true
end

--- Leaves fixed-edge mode and consumes the latched edges.
--- @param self the installed input state
function Input.exitFixedPhase(exclusive self: Input): nil
    self.fixed = false
    self.keysLatchedPressed = {}
    self.keysLatchedReleased = {}
    self.mouseButtonsLatchedPressed = {}
    self.mouseButtonsLatchedReleased = {}
    for index = 1, #self.padList do
        padExitFixed(self.padList[index])
    end
end

--- Returns the key one finger is tracked under.
---
--- A device and a finger are separate 64-bit identities, so two surfaces can
--- report the same finger number at the same moment and only the pair tells
--- them apart.
local function fingerKey(event: platformevents.TouchEvent): string
    return event.touchDevice .. ":" .. event.finger
end

--- Opens a device Input has not seen, or leaves the one it has.
local function connect(exclusive self: Input, id: integer): nil
    if self.pads[id] ~= nil then
        return
    end
    local backend = self.backend
    local pad = new Gamepad(
        id = id,
        connected = true,
        name = backend.name(id),
        guid = backend.guid(id),
        backend = backend,
        gateRead = function(layer: Layer?): boolean
            return self:canRead(layer)
        end,
        gateFixed = function(): boolean
            return self.fixed
        end,
        down = {},
        pressed = {},
        released = {},
        latchedPressed = {},
        latchedReleased = {},
        axisValues = {}
    )
    self.pads[id] = pad
    self.padList[#self.padList + 1] = pad
end

--- Retires a device, leaving any reference a game kept harmless.
local function disconnect(exclusive self: Input, id: integer): nil
    local pad = self.pads[id]
    if pad == nil then
        return
    end
    self.pads[id] = nil
    for index = 1, #self.padList do
        if self.padList[index] == pad then
            table.remove(self.padList, index)
            break
        end
    end
    padDisconnect(pad)
end

--- Folds one translated platform event into live and edge state.
--- @param self the input state to update
--- @param event the translated event, in host sequence order
function Input.handleEvent(exclusive self: Input, event: platformevents.Event): nil
    if event is platformevents.KeyEvent then
        self.modifierMask = event.modifiers
        if event.down then
            self.keysDown[event.physicalKey] = true
            if not event.repeated then
                self.keysPressed[event.physicalKey] = true
                self.keysLatchedPressed[event.physicalKey] = true
            end
        else
            self.keysDown[event.physicalKey] = nil
            self.keysReleased[event.physicalKey] = true
            self.keysLatchedReleased[event.physicalKey] = true
        end
    elseif event is platformevents.PointerButtonEvent then
        self.mouseX = event.x
        self.mouseY = event.y
        if event.down then
            self.mouseButtonsDown[event.button] = true
            self.mouseButtonsPressed[event.button] = true
            self.mouseButtonsLatchedPressed[event.button] = true
        else
            self.mouseButtonsDown[event.button] = nil
            self.mouseButtonsReleased[event.button] = true
            self.mouseButtonsLatchedReleased[event.button] = true
        end
    elseif event is platformevents.PointerMoveEvent then
        self.mouseX = event.x
        self.mouseY = event.y
        self.mouseDeltaX = self.mouseDeltaX + event.dx
        self.mouseDeltaY = self.mouseDeltaY + event.dy
    elseif event is platformevents.WheelEvent then
        self.mouseX = event.x
        self.mouseY = event.y
        self.wheelX = self.wheelX + event.wheelX
        self.wheelY = self.wheelY + event.wheelY
        self.wheelTicksX = self.wheelTicksX + event.wheelTicksX
        self.wheelTicksY = self.wheelTicksY + event.wheelTicksY
    elseif event is platformevents.TextEvent then
        self.text = self.text .. event.text
    elseif event is platformevents.TouchEvent then
        local key = fingerKey(event)
        if event.kind == "fingerUp" or event.kind == "fingerCanceled" then
            self.fingers[key] = nil
        else
            local touch = self.fingers[key]
            if touch == nil then
                touch = new Touch(
                    device = event.touchDevice,
                    finger = event.finger,
                    x = 0,
                    y = 0,
                    normalX = 0,
                    normalY = 0,
                    pressure = 0
                )
                self.fingers[key] = touch
            end
            touch.x = event.x
            touch.y = event.y
            touch.normalX = event.normalX
            touch.normalY = event.normalY
            touch.pressure = event.pressure
        end
    elseif event is platformevents.GamepadDeviceEvent then
        if event.kind == "gamepadAdded" then
            -- A reconnect under an identifier a previous device used has to
            -- retire that one first, or a reference a game kept would start
            -- answering for hardware it was never given.
            disconnect(self, event.which)
            connect(self, event.which)
        else
            disconnect(self, event.which)
        end
    elseif event is platformevents.GamepadButtonEvent then
        local pad = self.pads[event.which]
        if pad ~= nil then
            if event.down then
                pad.down[event.button] = true
                pad.pressed[event.button] = true
                pad.latchedPressed[event.button] = true
            else
                pad.down[event.button] = nil
                pad.released[event.button] = true
                pad.latchedReleased[event.button] = true
            end
        end
    elseif event is platformevents.GamepadAxisEvent then
        local pad = self.pads[event.which]
        if pad ~= nil then
            pad.axisValues[event.axis] = event.value
        end
    elseif event is platformevents.FocusEvent and not event.focused then
        releaseHeld(self)
    end
end

--- Returns whether a physical key is held.
--- @param self the input state to read
--- @param key the case-sensitive `winit` physical key name
--- @param layer the reading layer, or the base layer when omitted
--- @return whether the layer may read and the key is held
function Input.keyDown(borrows self: Input, key: string, layer: Layer?): boolean
    return self:canRead(layer) and self.keysDown[key] == true
end

--- Returns whether a physical key went down in the active edge tier.
--- @param self the input state to read
--- @param key the case-sensitive `winit` physical key name
--- @param layer the reading layer, or the base layer when omitted
--- @return whether the key was pressed
function Input.keyPressed(borrows self: Input, key: string, layer: Layer?): boolean
    local edges = self.fixed and self.keysLatchedPressed or self.keysPressed
    return self:canRead(layer) and edges[key] == true
end

--- Returns whether a physical key came up in the active edge tier.
--- @param self the input state to read
--- @param key the case-sensitive `winit` physical key name
--- @param layer the reading layer, or the base layer when omitted
--- @return whether the key was released
function Input.keyReleased(borrows self: Input, key: string, layer: Layer?): boolean
    local edges = self.fixed and self.keysLatchedReleased or self.keysReleased
    return self:canRead(layer) and edges[key] == true
end

--- Returns the modifier mask from the most recent key event.
--- @param self the input state to read
--- @return the host-defined modifier bit mask
function Input.modifiers(borrows self: Input): integer
    return self.modifierMask
end

--- Returns whether a pointer button is held.
--- @param self the input state to read
--- @param button a conventional name or positive host button number
--- @param layer the reading layer, or the base layer when omitted
--- @return whether the button is held
function Input.mouseDown(borrows self: Input, button: MouseButton | integer, layer: Layer?): boolean
    return self:canRead(layer) and self.mouseButtonsDown[mouseCode(button)] == true
end

--- Returns whether a pointer button went down in the active edge tier.
--- @param self the input state to read
--- @param button a conventional name or positive host button number
--- @param layer the reading layer, or the base layer when omitted
--- @return whether the button was pressed
function Input.mousePressed(borrows self: Input, button: MouseButton | integer, layer: Layer?): boolean
    local edges = self.fixed and self.mouseButtonsLatchedPressed or self.mouseButtonsPressed
    return self:canRead(layer) and edges[mouseCode(button)] == true
end

--- Returns whether a pointer button came up in the active edge tier.
--- @param self the input state to read
--- @param button a conventional name or positive host button number
--- @param layer the reading layer, or the base layer when omitted
--- @return whether the button was released
function Input.mouseReleased(borrows self: Input, button: MouseButton | integer, layer: Layer?): boolean
    local edges = self.fixed and self.mouseButtonsLatchedReleased or self.mouseButtonsReleased
    return self:canRead(layer) and edges[mouseCode(button)] == true
end

--- Pushes a capture layer.
--- @param self the input state to modify
--- @param name the non-empty diagnostic layer name
--- @param blocking whether the layer hides lower layers, defaulting to true
--- @return the new top layer
--- @raises when the layer name is empty
function Input.pushLayer(exclusive self: Input, name: string, blocking: boolean?): Layer
    if name == "" then
        error("input.pushLayer: name must not be empty", 2)
    end
    local before = readableFrom(self)
    local layer = new Layer(name = name, blocking = blocking ~= false, index = #self.layers + 1)
    self.layers[layer.index] = layer
    if readableFrom(self) ~= before then
        clearEdges(self)
    end

    return layer
end

--- Removes and returns the top layer, preserving the base layer.
--- @param self the input state to modify
--- @return the removed layer, or nil at the base
function Input.popLayer(exclusive self: Input): Layer?
    local count = #self.layers
    if count <= 1 then
        return nil
    end
    local before = readableFrom(self)
    local layer = self.layers[count]
    self.layers[count] = nil
    if readableFrom(self) ~= before then
        clearEdges(self)
    end

    return layer
end

--- Returns the top input layer.
--- @param self the input state to read
--- @return the top layer
function Input.topLayer(borrows self: Input): Layer
    return self.layers[#self.layers]
end

--- Reports whether a layer may read through the current capture stack.
--- @param self the input state to read
--- @param layer the layer to test, or the base layer when omitted
--- @return whether the layer is at or above the topmost blocking layer
function Input.canRead(borrows self: Input, layer: Layer?): boolean
    local index = layer ~= nil and layer.index or 1
    return index >= readableFrom(self)
end

--- Returns every finger currently on a touch surface.
---
--- The list is freshly built, and the records in it are Input's and are reused
--- as the finger moves, so a caller retaining one sees it change and a caller
--- wanting the position it had copies the numbers out.
--- @param self the input state to read
--- @param layer the reading layer, or the base layer when omitted
--- @return the fingers, in no defined order, and an empty list when the layer
---     may not read
function Input.touches(borrows self: Input, layer: Layer?): {Touch}
    if not self:canRead(layer) then
        return {}
    end
    local found: {Touch} = {}
    for _, touch in pairs(self.fingers) do
        found[#found + 1] = touch
    end

    return found
end

--- Returns the connected gamepads, in connection order.
--- @param self the input state to read
--- @return Input's own list. A caller may retain a gamepad from it and must not
---     edit the list.
function Input.gamepads(borrows self: Input): {Gamepad}
    return self.padList
end

--- Returns one connected gamepad by connection order.
--- @param self the input state to read
--- @param index the one-based position, defaulting to the first gamepad
--- @return the gamepad, or nil when that position is empty
function Input.gamepad(borrows self: Input, index: integer?): Gamepad?
    return self.padList[index ?? 1]
end

--- Returns one connected gamepad by device identifier.
--- @param self the input state to read
--- @param id the identifier a gamepad platform event carries in `which`
--- @return the gamepad, or nil once it has gone away
function Input.gamepadById(borrows self: Input, id: integer): Gamepad?
    return self.pads[id]
end

--- Reconciles the device list against what the platform currently reports.
---
--- Engine-owned. Hot plug arrives as events, so this is the safety net for a
--- stream that was not being drained rather than the ordinary path. It changes
--- the device list without emitting the events an ordinary attach produces,
--- because the world it would emit into is not this call's to reach.
--- @param self the input state to reconcile
function Input.refreshDevices(exclusive self: Input): nil
    local attached = self.backend.attached()
    local present: {[integer]: boolean} = {}
    for index = 1, #attached do
        present[attached[index]] = true
        connect(self, attached[index])
    end
    -- Copied first, because disconnecting rewrites the list under iteration.
    local known: {integer} = {}
    for index = 1, #self.padList do
        known[index] = self.padList[index].id
    end
    for index = 1, #known do
        if not present[known[index]] then
            disconnect(self, known[index])
        end
    end
end

--- Reads the gamepad source and folds what it saw into input state.
---
--- Engine-owned. The `tecs.PollGamepads` system `install` adds calls this once
--- a frame and dispatches what comes back, so ordinary game code reads
--- `gamepads` instead. A caller driving input without a world calls it directly
--- and may ignore the result.
--- @param self the input state to update
--- @return the translated events, already folded, in the order they arrived.
---     The list is the caller's and is empty on a frame with no device activity.
function Input.pollDevices(exclusive self: Input): {platformevents.Event}
    local produced: {platformevents.Event} = {}
    local backend = self.backend
    local buffer = self.padEvents
    local capacity = #buffer
    backend.poll()
    while true do
        local written = backend.drain(buffer)
        for index = 1, written do
            local observation = buffer[index]
            local event: platformevents.Event? = nil
            if observation.kind == gamepadbackend.KIND_ADDED then
                event = platformevents.gamepadDevice(true, observation.device)
            elseif observation.kind == gamepadbackend.KIND_REMOVED then
                event = platformevents.gamepadDevice(false, observation.device)
            elseif observation.kind == gamepadbackend.KIND_BUTTON_DOWN then
                event = platformevents.gamepadButton(true, observation.device, observation.code)
            elseif observation.kind == gamepadbackend.KIND_BUTTON_UP then
                event = platformevents.gamepadButton(false, observation.device, observation.code)
            elseif observation.kind == gamepadbackend.KIND_AXIS then
                event = platformevents.gamepadAxis(observation.device, observation.code, observation.value)
            end
            if event ~= nil then
                produced[#produced + 1] = event
                self:handleEvent(event)
            end
        end
        if written < capacity then
            break
        end
    end

    return produced
end

--- Retires every device and forgets every finger.
---
--- Engine-owned. A host tearing a world down early calls this; ordinary game
--- code must not. `tecs.application` does not call it, and nothing leaks when
--- nobody does: the state dies with its world, and the process-wide gamepad
--- source is deliberately left open here because another world may still be
--- reading it.
--- @param self the input state being shut down
function Input.destroy(exclusive self: Input): nil
    local pads = self.padList
    self.padList = {}
    self.pads = {}
    for index = 1, #pads do
        padDisconnect(pads[index])
    end
    self.fingers = {}
end

--- Returns whether a gamepad button is held.
--- @param self the gamepad to read
--- @param button a positional name or a positive backend button code
--- @param layer the reading layer, or the base layer when omitted
--- @return whether the layer may read and the button is held
--- @raises when the name is not one this module declares
function Gamepad.buttonDown(borrows self: Gamepad, button: GamepadButton | integer, layer: Layer?): boolean
    return self.gateRead(layer) and self.down[buttonCode(button)] == true
end

--- Returns whether a gamepad button went down in the active edge tier.
--- @param self the gamepad to read
--- @param button a positional name or a positive backend button code
--- @param layer the reading layer, or the base layer when omitted
--- @return whether the button was pressed
--- @raises when the name is not one this module declares
function Gamepad.buttonPressed(borrows self: Gamepad, button: GamepadButton | integer, layer: Layer?): boolean
    local edges = self.gateFixed() and self.latchedPressed or self.pressed
    return self.gateRead(layer) and edges[buttonCode(button)] == true
end

--- Returns whether a gamepad button came up in the active edge tier.
--- @param self the gamepad to read
--- @param button a positional name or a positive backend button code
--- @param layer the reading layer, or the base layer when omitted
--- @return whether the button was released
--- @raises when the name is not one this module declares
function Gamepad.buttonReleased(borrows self: Gamepad, button: GamepadButton | integer, layer: Layer?): boolean
    local edges = self.gateFixed() and self.latchedReleased or self.released
    return self.gateRead(layer) and edges[buttonCode(button)] == true
end

--- Returns an axis reading with the deadzone applied.
---
--- A stick runs -1 to 1 and a trigger runs zero to one. An axis no event has
--- mentioned, and one on a device that has gone away, reads as zero.
--- @param self the gamepad to read
--- @param axis an axis name or a positive backend axis code
--- @param deadzone the magnitude below which the reading is zero, defaulting to
---     0.15
--- @param layer the reading layer, or the base layer when omitted
--- @return the reading, and zero when the layer may not read
--- @raises when the name is not one this module declares
function Gamepad.axis(borrows self: Gamepad, axis: GamepadAxis | integer, deadzone: number?, layer: Layer?): number
    if not self.gateRead(layer) then
        return 0
    end
    local value = self.axisValues[axisCode(axis)] ?? 0
    local limit = deadzone ?? DEFAULT_DEADZONE
    if value < limit and value > -limit then
        return 0
    end

    return value
end

--- Reports whether the device has a button.
--- @param self the gamepad to ask
--- @param button a positional name or a positive backend button code
--- @return whether the platform says the device has it, and false once the
---     device has gone away
--- @raises when the name is not one this module declares
function Gamepad.hasButton(borrows self: Gamepad, button: GamepadButton | integer): boolean
    return self.connected and self.backend.hasButton(self.id, buttonCode(button))
end

--- Reports whether the device has an axis.
--- @param self the gamepad to ask
--- @param axis an axis name or a positive backend axis code
--- @return whether the platform says the device has it, and false once the
---     device has gone away
--- @raises when the name is not one this module declares
function Gamepad.hasAxis(borrows self: Gamepad, axis: GamepadAxis | integer): boolean
    return self.connected and self.backend.hasAxis(self.id, axisCode(axis))
end

--- Reports what the device says about its power source.
--- @param self the gamepad to ask
--- @return the state, which is `"unknown"` once the device has gone away
--- @return the battery percentage, and -1 when the device will not say
function Gamepad.power(borrows self: Gamepad): (gamepadbackend.PowerState, integer)
    if not self.connected then
        return "unknown", -1
    end
    local info = self.backend.info(self.id)

    return info.power, info.percent
end

--- Plays one rumble effect, replacing whatever the device was playing.
---
--- A zero or negative duration stops the device instead. Force feedback is not
--- available on every platform, so a false here is an ordinary answer rather
--- than a failure a game has to handle.
--- @param self the gamepad to shake
--- @param low the low-frequency motor strength from zero to one
--- @param high the high-frequency motor strength from zero to one
--- @param seconds how long the effect runs
--- @return whether the effect started, and false when the device has gone away
---     or the platform has no force feedback for it
function Gamepad.rumble(borrows self: Gamepad, low: number, high: number, seconds: number): boolean
    return self.connected and self.backend.rumble(self.id, low, high, seconds)
end

--- Installs and returns the input state owned by one world.
---
--- Three systems come with it. Two consume the fixed-step latched edges, and
--- `tecs.PollGamepads` runs in `Ingress`, ahead of everything a game schedules,
--- so a controller read anywhere in the frame answers from this frame.
---
--- This is the call that reaches real hardware. The platform's device source is
--- opened once per process and shared, because opening it per world would give
--- one controller two identities on two hot-plug streams; a build with no
--- `libtecsgamepad` gets the device-free source and says so once in the log.
--- @param world the world whose fixed steps consume latched edges
--- @param backend the gamepad source, defaulting to the process-wide platform
---     one
--- @return the new input state, also available through `of`
--- @raises when the world already owns input state
export function install(exclusive world: ecs.World, backend: gamepadbackend.Backend?): Input
    if world.resources[worldInputKey] ~= nil then
        error("input.install: world input state is already installed", 2)
    end
    local state = newInput(backend ?? devices())
    world.resources[worldInputKey] = state
    -- Compatibility surface: system names are what the debug server reports
    -- and what an agent selects on.
    world:addSystem({name = "tecs.PollGamepads", phase = ecs.phases.Ingress, run = assert(state.pollSystem),})
    world:addSystem({
        name = "tecs.EnterFixedInput",
        phase = ecs.phases.FixedFirst,
        run = assert(state.enterFixedSystem),
    })
    world:addSystem({name = "tecs.ExitFixedInput", phase = ecs.phases.FixedLast, run = assert(state.exitFixedSystem),})

    return state
end

--- Returns the input state installed in a world.
--- @param world the world to inspect
--- @return its input state, or nil when none is installed
export function of(borrows world: ecs.World): Input?
    return world.resources[worldInputKey]
end
